### TITLE ###
ListOfPairsUnpacksAcrossLoopVars
### DESCRIPTION ###
A multi-var for loop unpacks each element across its targets (runForLoopList),
so iterating a list of pairs binds both vars to the inner element type. The
checker used to assume two vars meant a map and fall back to binding the whole
inner list to the first var, which made `tz` a str[] and turned any str-typed
parameter it flowed into a provable - and therefore gating - mismatch. That
false error is what `rad check` reported on the shipped epoch tutorial.
### INPUT ###
pairs = [["Europe/London", "GB"], ["Asia/Tokyo", "JP"]]
for tz, flag in pairs:
    split(tz, "/")
    split(flag, "/")
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  pairs @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  pairs @ 2:17 -> str[][]
  split @ 3:5 -> dynamic
  tz @ 3:11 -> str
  split @ 4:5 -> dynamic
  flag @ 4:11 -> str

# Symbol types
  flag (loop): str
  pairs (local): str[][]
  tz (loop): str

# Diagnostics
  (none)
### TITLE ###
ThreeLoopVarsUnpackToo
### DESCRIPTION ###
The old two-var-only branch bound nothing at all for three or more targets,
leaving them untyped. Unpacking is arity-independent at runtime, so it is
here too.
### INPUT ###
rows = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]
for a, b, c in rows:
    abs(a)
    abs(b)
    abs(c)
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  rows @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  rows @ 2:16 -> int[][]
  abs @ 3:5 -> dynamic
  a @ 3:9 -> int
  abs @ 4:5 -> dynamic
  b @ 4:9 -> int
  abs @ 5:5 -> dynamic
  c @ 5:9 -> int

# Symbol types
  a (loop): int
  b (loop): int
  c (loop): int
  rows (local): int[][]

# Diagnostics
  (none)
### TITLE ###
MapStillIteratesAsKeyValue
### DESCRIPTION ###
The one shape where multiple vars don't unpack the element: a map iterates as
(key, value) pairs. This is the case the old code was written for, and it must
keep working - unpacking a map's keys across two vars would be wrong.
### INPUT ###
scores = {"a": 1, "b": 2}
for name, score in scores:
    split(name, ",")
    abs(score)
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  scores @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  scores @ 2:20 -> { str: int }
  split @ 3:5 -> dynamic
  name @ 3:11 -> str
  abs @ 4:5 -> dynamic
  score @ 4:9 -> int

# Symbol types
  name (loop): str
  score (loop): int
  scores (local): { str: int }

# Diagnostics
  (none)
### TITLE ###
UnknownIterableStaysPermissive
### DESCRIPTION ###
Negative case: with nothing known about the element there is nothing to
distribute, so every target stays permissive rather than being pinned to a
guess the loop would then be diagnosed against.
### INPUT ###
data = parse_json("[]")
for a, b in data:
    abs(a)
    abs(b)
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  data @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  parse_json @ 1:8 -> dynamic
  data @ 2:13 -> any
  abs @ 3:5 -> dynamic
  a @ 3:9 -> dynamic
  abs @ 4:5 -> dynamic
  b @ 4:9 -> dynamic

# Symbol types
  a (loop): dynamic
  b (loop): dynamic
  data (local): any

# Diagnostics
  (none)
